<p>Most people arrive at this question having already priced both, which sets the comparison up the wrong way round. PRP and a hair transplant are not a cheaper and a dearer option for the same problem. They act at different points in the life of a follicle, and what decides between them is the point your scalp has actually reached.</p>

<p>A follicle that is thinning is still there. A follicle that has gone is not. Nearly everything below follows from that one distinction, and it is the reason a clinic that offers you both without examining your scalp is guessing.</p>

<h2 id="what-prp-is-asked-to-do">What PRP is asked to do</h2>

<p>Platelet-rich plasma is made from your own blood. A sample is drawn, spun to concentrate the platelet fraction, and that fraction is injected into the thinning areas. Platelets carry growth factors, and the rationale is that delivering them into the scalp supports follicles that are struggling.</p>

<p>The word doing the work in that sentence is <em>supports</em>. PRP is asked to act on follicles that still exist and are miniaturising — producing a hair each cycle, but a progressively finer, shorter and less pigmented one, until eventually the hair is too fine to contribute anything visible. Miniaturisation is the central mechanism of pattern hair loss (<a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/male-pattern-hair-loss" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet: male pattern hair loss</a>), and it is a gradual process, not a switch.</p>

<p>Where there is no follicle left, there is nothing for PRP to support. That is not a failure of the treatment; it is the boundary of what it is for. The <a href="/hair-prp-treatment-in-gurgaon">PRP treatment page sets out how a course is structured</a>, and <a href="/blog/does-prp-work-for-hair-loss">what the published evidence does and does not show</a> is worth reading before you commit to a course.</p>

<h2 id="what-a-transplant-does">What a hair transplant does instead</h2>

<p>A transplant does not treat follicles. It moves them. Follicular units are taken from the back and sides, where hair is largely insensitive to the hormonal process driving pattern loss, and placed into areas where density has already gone. The relocated hair keeps the behaviour of where it came from, which is why it keeps growing in its new position.</p>

<p>This is redistribution, not creation. You do not end up with more hair than you started with — you end up with the same hair arranged differently, and the donor supply that funded the move is spent permanently. That is the whole argument of <a href="/blog/donor-area-limits-hair-transplant">why the donor area behaves like a one-time budget</a>, and it is why surgery is planned around a lifetime rather than a season. If you are weighing up the procedure itself, <a href="/hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">how a hair transplant is planned and performed</a> covers the mechanics.</p>

<p>So a transplant answers a question PRP cannot touch: what to do about skin where the follicles are gone. And PRP answers one surgery cannot: what to do about follicles that are still alive but failing.</p>

<h2 id="neither-treats-the-cause">Neither one addresses what is driving the loss</h2>

<p>This is the part that gets left out of most comparisons. Pattern hair loss is an inherited sensitivity of certain follicles to androgens. Injecting plasma does not alter that sensitivity. Neither does moving follicles around the scalp. The process carries on underneath both treatments, on its own timetable.</p>

<p>The practical consequence is that a transplant does not stop shedding elsewhere. Native hairs around the grafts stay on their original trajectory, which is why a result that looked complete at two years can look patchy at eight — the argument set out in <a href="/blog/is-a-hair-transplant-permanent">whether a transplant is genuinely permanent</a>. And PRP, if it helps, helps only for as long as it is continued. Neither is a cure, and a clinic describing either as one has told you something about the clinic.</p>

<h2 id="how-the-difference-is-assessed">How miniaturisation and complete loss are told apart</h2>

<p>You cannot do this reliably in a mirror, and neither can a clinic from a photograph sent on WhatsApp. What an examination looks at:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Hair shaft diameter under magnification.</strong> In an area that is miniaturising, hairs of very different thicknesses sit side by side. Uniform thickness suggests the area is not currently affected; wide variation suggests it is.</li>
  <li><strong>Follicular openings.</strong> Trichoscopy can show whether openings are still present and occupied by fine hairs, or whether the skin has become smooth and empty. Empty skin is the point at which PRP stops being a sensible offer.</li>
  <li><strong>Density counts across zones.</strong> Front, mid-scalp, crown and donor are counted separately, because the picture is rarely uniform and a plan has to be built zone by zone.</li>
  <li><strong>A pull test and a look at the whole scalp.</strong> Diffuse shedding from everywhere behaves differently from patterned thinning, and mistaking one for the other changes everything that follows.</li>
  <li><strong>Old photographs.</strong> What your hairline looked like three years ago is genuinely diagnostic, and no examination substitutes for it. Bring them.</li>
</ul>

<p>Honesty about the limits here matters. Scalps rarely divide neatly into one category or the other. A crown can be bare at its centre and miniaturising at its edges, and the sensible answer for that scalp may involve both approaches in a sequence, or neither for the moment. This is also why a stage on the <a href="/blog/norwood-scale-explained">Norwood scale is a poor basis for a treatment decision</a> — it describes shape, not follicular status.</p>

<h2 id="prp-on-a-bald-crown">What happens when PRP is bought for a bald crown</h2>

<p>Very little, and that is predictable before the first needle. A patch of scalp that has been smooth for years has no follicles left to stimulate, so a course of injections into it spends several months and produces no change anyone can see.</p>

<p>Two things follow, and both are avoidable. The first is that the patient concludes PRP does not work, when the accurate conclusion is that it was the wrong treatment for that skin. The second is that the months spent were months in which the areas that <em>were</em> still miniaturising went untreated. That is the real cost, and it does not appear on any invoice.</p>

<p>If a clinic has offered you PRP for an area that has been bare for years, the questions in <a href="/blog/what-to-ask-before-prp-in-gurgaon">what to ask before paying for a course of PRP</a> are the ones that expose it. The same applies to the newer injectables sold at higher prices: which product is better is a separate question from whether any injectable is indicated for your scalp at all.</p>

