<h2 id="prp-around-a-transplant">Using PRP around a transplant</h2>

<p>PRP is frequently offered alongside surgery, sometimes injected during the procedure and sometimes in the weeks afterwards. Three things are hoped for: that grafts settle and start growing more reliably, that post-operative shedding of native hair is reduced, and that the untransplanted hair between and behind the grafts is supported.</p>

<p>Those are reasonable hopes. They are not established facts. The published work is small in scale, preparation methods differ between clinics so results are hard to compare, and no standard protocol exists for dose or timing. If PRP around surgery is presented to you as something that improves graft survival by a stated amount, that number has been invented.</p>

<p>The honest framing is that it may help, it is unlikely to do harm, and it is an optional addition rather than part of the operation. It is worth separating it from the shedding that is expected anyway — <a href="/blog/shedding-and-shock-loss-after-hair-transplant">what normal post-transplant shedding looks like</a> is a useful baseline, because otherwise any shedding gets read as PRP having failed.</p>

<h2 id="who-needs-neither-yet">The people who need neither procedure yet</h2>

<p>This is the group most likely to be sold something, because they arrive worried and their scalps look alarming.</p>

<h3>Active diffuse shedding</h3>
<p>Hair coming out in handfuls from all over the scalp, usually two to three months after an illness, a surgery, a crash diet, childbirth or severe stress, is typically telogen effluvium — and it usually recovers without treatment once the trigger has passed (<a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/telogen-effluvium" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet: telogen effluvium</a>). Injecting or operating into an active shed treats the wrong problem. <a href="/blog/telogen-effluvium-vs-pattern-hair-loss">Telling temporary shedding apart from pattern loss</a> is the first job.</p>

<h3>An unstable or fast-moving pattern</h3>
<p>A pattern that has visibly changed in the past year has not settled. Operating into it produces an island of transplanted hair with a widening gap around it, and this is one of the situations covered in <a href="/blog/who-should-not-have-a-hair-transplant">when surgery is the wrong answer</a>. Medical treatment and a review with fixed-angle photographs is the sensible sequence.</p>

<h3>An untreated medical cause</h3>
<p>Thyroid disease, iron deficiency, a new medication and several other conditions cause hair loss that responds to treating the condition rather than the hair (<a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hair-loss/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS: hair loss</a>). Which tests are actually worth doing is set out in <a href="/blog/blood-tests-for-hair-fall">the blood tests that matter for hair fall</a>. In women this investigation runs wider still and comes before any discussion of procedures, as <a href="/blog/female-pattern-hair-loss-in-women">the workup for female pattern hair loss</a> explains.</p>

<h2 id="where-medical-treatment-sits">Where medicines sit alongside either choice</h2>

<p>Both procedures work better inside a plan that includes medical treatment, because medical treatment is the only part of the plan that acts on the process itself. That does not make it right for everyone. These are prescription medicines with side effects worth discussing properly, some of them relevant to fertility and pregnancy, and the decision belongs in a conversation with a doctor who has examined you and taken a history — not in a checkout flow. <a href="/blog/minoxidil-and-finasteride-in-india">How minoxidil and finasteride are regulated in India</a> covers what that conversation should include.</p>

<p>If you want the short version: find out what your scalp is doing before you choose between two treatments for different problems. An <a href="/hair-fall-treatment-in-gurgaon">assessment of what is causing your hair fall</a> answers that, and you can read <a href="/dr-nyra">about the dermatologist who carries out the examination</a> before you book.</p>

